# L §4.2 Date arithmetic: "A period moves by an int of its own periods ... or by a duration that is a whole number
# of them (`y + 5 years` is `y + 5`, `m + 1 year` is `m + 12`)".  So 2026-03 + 1 month is 2026-04, 2026-03 + 1 year
# is 2027-03, 2026 + 5 years is 2031, 2026-03 - 2 years is 2024-03, and the quarter holding 2026-02 plus 6 months
# is two quarters on, the quarter holding 2026-08-01 (the figures are rows, L §7.1).  `y + n` with n = 6 months is not a whole number of years: the count shows only at
# the key, so it is refused there (L §8.4: RUN-DOMAIN).
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"

input n: num month
input five: num year
input two: num year
input six: num month

def a[m in months]: month = m + 1 month
def b[m in months]: month = m + 1 year
def c[y in years]: year = y + five
def d[m in months]: month = m - two
def e[m in months]: quarter = quarter_of(m) + six
def f[y in years]: year = y + n

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  n = 6 month
  five = 5 year
  two = 2 year
  six = 6 month

ask q1: a[2026-03], b[2026-03], c[2026], d[2026-03]
ask q2: e[2026-02] == quarter_of(2026-08-01)
ask q3: f[2026]
=== expect
q1: (2026-04, 2027-03, 2031, 2024-03)
  cut 2026-12-31
q2: true
  cut 2026-12-31
q3: refused: RUN-DOMAIN ...
  cut 2026-12-31
